WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.773
Decision 2: the MCP server boundary.

2
00:00:03.053 --> 00:00:05.805
Week 3 taught you to build MCP servers.

3
00:00:06.085 --> 00:00:12.869
For this project the server is the boundary between your agent and the tracker, and the place where least privilege lives.

4
00:00:13.149 --> 00:00:15.176
Start with resources and read tools.

5
00:00:15.456 --> 00:00:31.904
Something like list_changed_issues(since, until) that returns compact records (number, title, state, labels, assignee, updated time, a truncated body) rather than raw API payloads; get_issue(number) with comments for the cases that need detail;

6
00:00:32.184 --> 00:00:37.112
perhaps a resource for the project’s configuration (milestones, label meanings).

7
00:00:37.392 --> 00:00:47.568
Return natural-language fields and sensible page sizes, as Studio 1’s tool-design lesson recommended, and do the date arithmetic in the server, not in the model.

8
00:00:47.848 --> 00:00:48.957
Then the write path.

9
00:00:49.237 --> 00:00:52.992
The agent should not hold a general “post anything anywhere” tool.

10
00:00:53.272 --> 00:01:03.085
One option: the agent’s only write is save_draft(text), and posting happens in your application after a human approves, through code the model never calls.

11
00:01:03.365 --> 00:01:09.893
Another: a post_status tool on the server that only accepts the configured channel and is gated by approval.

12
00:01:10.173 --> 00:01:12.200
Decide which, and write down why.

13
00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:22.016
The MCP tools specification expects a human able to deny tool invocations; your design should make that the normal path, not an exception.

14
00:01:22.296 --> 00:01:23.768
Finally, credentials.

15
00:01:24.048 --> 00:01:32.432
For GitHub, a fine-grained personal access token can be limited to specific repositories and specific permissions, with an expiry.

16
00:01:32.712 --> 00:01:44.723
A read-only token on one repository is enough for the default project; if you post back to GitHub (a discussion or a comment), give that one action its own narrowly scoped token.

17
00:01:45.003 --> 00:01:49.845
Keep tokens in environment variables, never in fixtures, logs, or prompts.
